# Goal 10: Fix logb — off-by-one for subnormal doubles

## Function
`logb` (double precision)

## Source Files
- C source: `/home/leochanj/Desktop/libmcs/libm/mathd/logbd.c`
- Rust source: `/home/leochanj/Desktop/libmcs/newexp/rust-s5/src/mathd.rs` (fn `logbd` at line 3103)

## Problem
MISMATCH — `logb` returns wrong exponents for subnormal numbers:
- `logb 0x1p-1074`: C=`-0x1.0c8p+10` (-1074.0), Rust=`-0x1.0ccp+10` (-1075.0)
- `logb 0x1p-1073`: C=`-0x1.0c4p+10` (-1073.0), Rust=`-0x1.0c8p+10` (-1074.0)

Off by one, same pattern as ilogb. Likely shares the same subnormal normalization bug.

## What Needs to Change
Compare the subnormal handling in Rust `logbd` with the C source. The bit-counting or normalization shift for subnormals is off by one.

## Success Criteria
- `logb 0x1p-1074` returns `-0x1.0c8p+10` (-1074.0)
- `logb 0x1p-1073` returns `-0x1.0c4p+10` (-1073.0)
- All logb test cases match C output exactly
